{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py start .trellis/tasks/09-20-teardown-cleanup-errors --allow-empty-context && python3 ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode phase --step 2.1 --platform codex && sed -n '1,220p' .trellis/tasks/09-20-teardown-cleanup-errors/prd.md && sed -n '1,180p' .trellis/tasks/09-20-teardown-cleanup-errors/design.md && sed -n '1,180p' .trellis/tasks/09-20-teardown-cleanup-errors/implement.md\"","aggregated_output":"\u001b[1;33mNote: no checked-out branch (detached HEAD, or not a git repository); task branch not recorded.\u001b[0m\n\u001b[0;32m✓ Current task set to: .trellis/tasks/09-20-teardown-cleanup-errors\u001b[0m\nSource: session:codex_01a0be86-c8da-7260-b117-0d776259b771\n\u001b[0;32m✓ Status: planning → in_progress\u001b[0m\n\n\u001b[0;34mThe hook will now inject context from this task's jsonl files.\u001b[0m\n#### 2.1 Implement `[required · repeatable]`\n\n\nSpawn the implement sub-agent:\n\n- **Agent type**: `trellis-implement`\n- **Task description**: Implement the reviewed task artifacts, consulting materials under `{TASK_DIR}/research/`; finish by running project lint and type-check\n- **Dispatch prompt guard**: The prompt MUST start with `Active task: <task path>`, then tell the spawned agent it is already the `trellis-implement` sub-agent and must implement directly, not spawn another `trellis-implement` / `trellis-check`.\n\nThe platform hook/plugin auto-handles:\n- Reads `implement.jsonl` and injects referenced spec/research files into the agent prompt\n- Injects `prd.md`, `design.md` if present, and `implement.md` if present\n- For Codex, `SubagentStart` supplies native context injection; the agent profile keeps child-side loading as the fallback\n# Teardown cleanup errors\n\n## Goal\n\nRun every Flask teardown callback and signal before surfacing teardown errors.\n\n## Confirmed Facts\n\n- Request teardown callbacks and the request teardown signal are invoked by\n  `Flask.do_teardown_request`.\n- Application-context teardown callbacks and the application-context teardown\n  signal are invoked by `Flask.do_teardown_appcontext`.\n- `AppContext.pop` currently restores its context-local token after running\n  application teardown, so an application teardown error can prevent that\n  restoration.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Attempt every registered request teardown callback, then send the request\n  teardown signal, even if earlier teardown work raises.\n- Attempt every registered application-context teardown callback, then send\n  the application-context teardown signal, even if earlier teardown work\n  raises.\n- Raise collected teardown errors only after the applicable cleanup work has\n  run. On Python versions with exception groups, retain every collected error;\n  on older versions, raise the first error.\n- Restore context-local state before any teardown error leaves\n  `AppContext.pop`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Request teardown callbacks after a failure and its signal are observed.\n- [ ] Application teardown callbacks after a failure and its signal are\n  observed.\n- [ ] The resulting error retains the teardown failures on Python 3.11+.\n- [ ] `flask.current_app` is unavailable after a failing context pop.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Changing callback registration order, callback signatures, or unrelated\n  signal behavior.\n- Changing non-teardown error propagation.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Keep `prd.md` focused on requirements, constraints, and acceptance criteria.\n# Design: Teardown cleanup errors\n\nUse one private Flask helper to run a sequence of teardown operations while\ncollecting failures. Each teardown path supplies its registered callbacks and\nits final signal send as operations, preserving the existing callback order and\nthe signal's position after callbacks. The helper raises an exception group on\nPython 3.11+ and the first collected exception on Python 3.10.\n\n`AppContext.pop` will retain request-teardown failures long enough to run\napplication teardown, reset the context-local token in a `finally` block, then\nraise the collected failures. This is limited to teardown sequencing; normal\nrequest dispatch and signal behavior are unchanged.\n# Implementation plan\n\n1. Add the shared teardown operation executor to `src/flask/app.py` and use it\n   for request and application-context teardown callbacks plus their signals.\n2. Ensure `src/flask/ctx.py` resets the app context token before propagating a\n   request or application teardown failure.\n3. Add focused tests for continuation, error aggregation, signals, and\n   context-local restoration.\n4. Run the provisioned app-context tests and the focused test modules.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
