{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,320p' .agents/skills/trellis-check/SKILL.md && grep -nE \\\"\\\\[tool\\\\.(ruff|mypy|pytest)|ruff|mypy|pyright\\\" pyproject.toml && git diff --check && git diff -- src/flask/app.py src/flask/ctx.py tests/test_appctx.py\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: trellis-check\ndescription: \"Comprehensive quality verification: spec compliance, lint, type-check, tests, cross-layer data flow, code reuse, and consistency checks. Use when code is written and needs quality verification, before committing changes, or to catch context drift during long sessions.\"\n---\n\n# Code Quality Check\n\nComprehensive quality verification for recently written code. Combines spec compliance, cross-layer safety, and pre-commit checks.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Identify What Changed\n\n```bash\ngit diff --name-only HEAD\ngit status\n```\n\n## Step 2: Read Task Artifacts and Applicable Specs\n\nRead the current task artifacts in order:\n\n- `prd.md`\n- `design.md` if present\n- `implement.md` if present\n\n```bash\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode packages\n```\n\nFor each changed package/layer, read the spec index and follow its **Quality Check** section:\n\n```bash\ncat .trellis/spec/<package>/<layer>/index.md\n```\n\nRead the specific guideline files referenced — the index is a pointer, not the goal.\n\n## Step 3: Run Project Checks\n\nRun the project's lint, type-check, and test commands. Fix any failures before proceeding.\n\n## Step 4: Review Against Checklist\n\n### Code Quality\n\n- [ ] Linter passes?\n- [ ] Type checker passes (if applicable)?\n- [ ] Tests pass?\n- [ ] No debug logging left in?\n- [ ] No suppressed warnings or type-safety bypasses?\n\n### Test Coverage\n\n- [ ] New function → unit test added?\n- [ ] Bug fix → regression test added?\n- [ ] Changed behavior → existing tests updated?\n\n### Spec Sync\n\n- [ ] Does `.trellis/spec/` need updates? (new patterns, conventions, lessons learned)\n\n> \"If I fixed a bug or discovered something non-obvious, should I document it so future me won't hit the same issue?\" → If YES, update the relevant spec doc.\n\n### Scope Discipline\n\n- [ ] Any tidying of code the task did not require?\n- [ ] Any abstraction, config or extension point added for a case that does not exist yet?\n- [ ] Any speculative fallback for a state that cannot occur?\n- [ ] Any file changed that the acceptance criteria do not mention?\n- [ ] Any workaround added at the caller instead of a fix where the behavior actually lives?\n\n## Step 5: Cross-Layer Dimensions (if applicable)\n\nSkip this step if your change is confined to a single layer.\n\n### A. Data Flow (changes touch 3+ layers)\n\n- [ ] Read flow traces correctly: Storage → Service → API → UI\n- [ ] Write flow traces correctly: UI → API → Service → Storage\n- [ ] Types/schemas correctly passed between layers?\n- [ ] Errors properly propagated to caller?\n\n### B. Code Reuse (modifying constants, creating utilities)\n\n- [ ] Searched for existing similar code before creating new?\n  ```bash\n  grep -r \"pattern\" src/\n  ```\n- [ ] If the same value repeats, does it represent one stable concept whose callers must change together? Extract only then — two literals that merely happen to match today should stay separate.\n- [ ] After batch modification, all occurrences updated?\n\n### C. Import/Dependency (creating new files)\n\n- [ ] Correct import paths (relative vs absolute)?\n- [ ] No circular dependencies?\n\n### D. Same-Layer Consistency\n\n- [ ] Other places using the same concept are consistent?\n\n---\n\n## Step 6: Report and Fix\n\nReport every violation you find. Then:\n\n- Mechanical and local (lint nit, missing type, wrong import, dead branch, failing assertion) → fix in place, then re-run project checks.\n- Design or judgment (naming a shared concept, moving a module boundary, changing a public interface, reassigning where behavior lives) → record the evidence and your recommendation, and stop. Do not rewrite it silently.\n\nIf a fix would touch files outside the current task's scope, say so and stop instead of widening the change.\n38:    \"ruff\",\n67:    \"mypy\",\n68:    \"pyright\",\n107:[tool.pytest.ini_options]\n127:[tool.mypy]\n134:[[tool.mypy.overrides]]\n143:[tool.pyright]\n148:[tool.ruff]\n154:[tool.ruff.lint]\n164:[tool.ruff.lint.isort]\n244:    [\"mypy\"],\n245:    [\"pyright\"],\ndiff --git a/src/flask/app.py b/src/flask/app.py\nindex 9a6fdd4..5f6735c 100644\n--- a/src/flask/app.py\n+++ b/src/flask/app.py\n@@ -31,6 +31,8 @@ from werkzeug.wsgi import get_host\n from . import cli\n from . import typing as ft\n from .ctx import AppContext\n+from .ctx import _call_teardown_functions\n+from .ctx import _raise_teardown_errors\n from .globals import _cv_app\n from .globals import app_ctx\n from .globals import g\n@@ -1433,12 +1435,17 @@ class Flask(App):\n         .. versionchanged:: 0.9\n             Added the ``exc`` argument.\n         \"\"\"\n-        for name in chain(ctx.request.blueprints, (None,)):\n-            if name in self.teardown_request_funcs:\n-                for func in reversed(self.teardown_request_funcs[name]):\n-                    self.ensure_sync(func)(exc)\n+        def teardown_functions() -> t.Iterator[t.Callable[[], t.Any]]:\n+            for name in chain(ctx.request.blueprints, (None,)):\n+                if name in self.teardown_request_funcs:\n+                    for func in reversed(self.teardown_request_funcs[name]):\n+                        yield lambda func=func: self.ensure_sync(func)(exc)\n+\n+            yield lambda: request_tearing_down.send(\n+                self, _async_wrapper=self.ensure_sync, exc=exc\n+            )\n \n-        request_tearing_down.send(self, _async_wrapper=self.ensure_sync, exc=exc)\n+        _raise_teardown_errors(_call_teardown_functions(teardown_functions()))\n \n     def do_teardown_appcontext(\n         self, ctx: AppContext, exc: BaseException | None = None\n@@ -1454,10 +1461,15 @@ class Flask(App):\n \n         .. versionadded:: 0.9\n         \"\"\"\n-        for func in reversed(self.teardown_appcontext_funcs):\n-            self.ensure_sync(func)(exc)\n+        def teardown_functions() -> t.Iterator[t.Callable[[], t.Any]]:\n+            for func in reversed(self.teardown_appcontext_funcs):\n+                yield lambda func=func: self.ensure_sync(func)(exc)\n+\n+            yield lambda: appcontext_tearing_down.send(\n+                self, _async_wrapper=self.ensure_sync, exc=exc\n+            )\n \n-        appcontext_tearing_down.send(self, _async_wrapper=self.ensure_sync, exc=exc)\n+        _raise_teardown_errors(_call_teardown_functions(teardown_functions()))\n \n     def app_context(self) -> AppContext:\n         \"\"\"Create an :class:`.AppContext`. When the context is pushed,\ndiff --git a/src/flask/ctx.py b/src/flask/ctx.py\nindex 8876efd..e5cab39 100644\n--- a/src/flask/ctx.py\n+++ b/src/flask/ctx.py\n@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@\n from __future__ import annotations\n \n import contextvars\n+import sys\n import typing as t\n from functools import update_wrapper\n from types import TracebackType\n@@ -26,6 +27,40 @@ if t.TYPE_CHECKING:\n _sentinel = object()\n \n \n+def _call_teardown_functions(\n+    funcs: t.Iterable[t.Callable[[], t.Any]],\n+) -> list[BaseException]:\n+    \"\"\"Call teardown functions, collecting errors so cleanup can continue.\"\"\"\n+    errors = []\n+\n+    for func in funcs:\n+        try:\n+            func()\n+        except BaseException as e:\n+            errors.append(e)\n+\n+    return errors\n+\n+\n+def _raise_teardown_errors(errors: list[BaseException]) -> None:\n+    \"\"\"Raise teardown errors after all cleanup functions have run.\"\"\"\n+    if not errors:\n+        return\n+\n+    if sys.version_info >= (3, 11):\n+        raise BaseExceptionGroup(\"Errors occurred during teardown\", errors)\n+\n+    raise errors[0]\n+\n+\n+def _get_teardown_errors(error: BaseException) -> list[BaseException]:\n+    \"\"\"Unpack an error raised by :func:`_raise_teardown_errors`.\"\"\"\n+    if sys.version_info >= (3, 11) and isinstance(error, BaseExceptionGroup):\n+        return list(error.exceptions)\n+\n+    return [error]\n+\n+\n class _AppCtxGlobals:\n     \"\"\"A plain object. Used as a namespace for storing data during an\n     application context.\n@@ -482,15 +517,30 @@ class AppContext:\n         if self._push_count > 0:\n             return\n \n+        errors = []\n+\n         try:\n             if self._request is not None:\n-                self.app.do_teardown_request(self, exc)\n-                self._request.close()\n+                try:\n+                    self.app.do_teardown_request(self, exc)\n+                except BaseException as e:\n+                    errors.extend(_get_teardown_errors(e))\n+\n+                try:\n+                    self._request.close()\n+                except BaseException as e:\n+                    errors.append(e)\n         finally:\n-            self.app.do_teardown_appcontext(self, exc)\n-            _cv_app.reset(self._cv_token)\n-            self._cv_token = None\n-            appcontext_popped.send(self.app, _async_wrapper=self.app.ensure_sync)\n+            try:\n+                self.app.do_teardown_appcontext(self, exc)\n+            except BaseException as e:\n+                errors.extend(_get_teardown_errors(e))\n+            finally:\n+                _cv_app.reset(self._cv_token)\n+                self._cv_token = None\n+                appcontext_popped.send(self.app, _async_wrapper=self.app.ensure_sync)\n+\n+        _raise_teardown_errors(errors)\n \n     def __enter__(self) -> te.Self:\n         self.push()\ndiff --git a/tests/test_appctx.py b/tests/test_appctx.py\nindex a537b05..e7afc62 100644\n--- a/tests/test_appctx.py\n+++ b/tests/test_appctx.py\n@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@\n+import sys\n+\n import pytest\n \n import flask\n@@ -53,6 +55,82 @@ def test_app_tearing_down(app):\n     assert cleanup_stuff == [None]\n \n \n+def test_app_teardown_runs_all_callbacks_and_signal_after_error(app):\n+    calls = []\n+\n+    @app.teardown_appcontext\n+    def first(exc):\n+        calls.append(\"first\")\n+        raise ValueError(\"first\")\n+\n+    @app.teardown_appcontext\n+    def second(exc):\n+        calls.append(\"second\")\n+        raise RuntimeError(\"second\")\n+\n+    def record_signal(sender, exc):\n+        calls.append(\"signal\")\n+\n+    flask.appcontext_tearing_down.connect(record_signal, app)\n+    try:\n+        with pytest.raises(BaseException) as info:\n+            with app.app_context():\n+                pass\n+    finally:\n+        flask.appcontext_tearing_down.disconnect(record_signal, app)\n+\n+    assert calls == [\"second\", \"first\", \"signal\"]\n+    assert not flask.current_app\n+\n+    if sys.version_info >= (3, 11):\n+        assert isinstance(info.value, BaseExceptionGroup)\n+        assert [type(error) for error in info.value.exceptions] == [\n+            RuntimeError,\n+            ValueError,\n+        ]\n+        assert [str(error) for error in info.value.exceptions] == [\"second\", \"first\"]\n+    else:\n+        assert isinstance(info.value, RuntimeError)\n+\n+\n+def test_request_teardown_runs_all_callbacks_and_signal_after_error(app):\n+    calls = []\n+\n+    @app.teardown_request\n+    def first(exc):\n+        calls.append(\"first\")\n+        raise ValueError(\"first\")\n+\n+    @app.teardown_request\n+    def second(exc):\n+        calls.append(\"second\")\n+        raise RuntimeError(\"second\")\n+\n+    def record_signal(sender, exc):\n+        calls.append(\"signal\")\n+\n+    flask.request_tearing_down.connect(record_signal, app)\n+    try:\n+        with pytest.raises(BaseException) as info:\n+            with app.test_request_context():\n+                pass\n+    finally:\n+        flask.request_tearing_down.disconnect(record_signal, app)\n+\n+    assert calls == [\"second\", \"first\", \"signal\"]\n+    assert not flask.current_app\n+\n+    if sys.version_info >= (3, 11):\n+        assert isinstance(info.value, BaseExceptionGroup)\n+        assert [type(error) for error in info.value.exceptions] == [\n+            RuntimeError,\n+            ValueError,\n+        ]\n+        assert [str(error) for error in info.value.exceptions] == [\"second\", \"first\"]\n+    else:\n+        assert isinstance(info.value, RuntimeError)\n+\n+\n def test_app_tearing_down_with_previous_exception(app):\n     cleanup_stuff = []\n \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
